{
  "case": "rs_parity_rrg_stockcharts",
  "kind": "rs_parity",
  "note": "Public-method parity for the UCT RS ROTATION columns (engineering map 2026-11-08 unit 3-13, decision D1, §6), read by tests/test_rs_phase3_parity.py against api/services/relative_strength.rrg_columns (the Terminal's RRG_METHOD: SMA 10 / 5, here on weekly closes). StockCharts publishes no RRG data API and its RS-Ratio / RS-Momentum normalisation is proprietary, so the reference is a PERSON'S READING of the StockCharts RRG for the 11 SPDR sector ETFs on the 12 weekly dates below (132 cells). Until every `stockcharts_quadrant` is filled, the agreement test is a strict xfail ('awaiting owner StockCharts readings'). Once filled, it computes our quadrant for each cell from `weekly_closes` and requires agreement ≥ `agreement_floor` (0.85). If it misses, the failure prints the measured agreement: record that number, mark the test xfail with it, and REOPEN D1 (the Terminal and the chart then switch together to the plan's §7.14 formula, in one unit). The floor is never edited. `weekly_closes` are split- and dividend-adjusted daily closes from Yahoo Finance (yfinance auto_adjust=True, fetched 2026-11-07), the last session of each ISO week keyed by that week's Friday (the app's isoWeekFriday convention). The readings are StockCharts'; nothing in this file is our output.",
  "instructions": [
    "Open the StockCharts RRG chart (stockcharts.com, Charts & Tools, RRG Charts). Pick the 'S&P Sector ETFs' universe, or type the 11 tickers in `tickers`.",
    "Set the BENCHMARK to SPY (StockCharts' sector default is $SPX; SPY is what our side uses).",
    "Set the timeframe to WEEKLY and the tail length to 12 weeks.",
    "Move the end date to the LAST date in `dates` (2026-11-06). Each sector's tail then has one dot per date in `dates`, oldest first.",
    "For every ticker and date, write the quadrant its dot sits in into readings[date][ticker].stockcharts_quadrant: exactly one of 'Leading', 'Weakening', 'Lagging', 'Improving'. Hovering a dot shows its date and both values; above 100 on both = Leading, ratio above and momentum below = Weakening, both below = Lagging, ratio below and momentum above = Improving.",
    "A dot exactly on a 100 line counts as the upper side (as ours does); mention it in reference.reader_notes.",
    "Fill reference.reader and reference.read_on (the date you read the chart). Change nothing else, and never `agreement_floor`."
  ],
